\subsection{Fully Connected Layers with BinConv}
\label{sec:fc_binconv}

\iffalse
When designing the BinConv architecture, we deliberately avoided using fully connected layers for two main reasons. First, fully connected layers introduce significantly more trainable parameters compared to convolutional layers, especially given that the model's output lies in a high-dimensional space. Second, due to the design of CBE and the way the loss is computed during training, using a fully connected layer, particularly as the final layer, may hinder BinConv’s ability to forecast values on the test set that fall outside the range observed during training. This limitation arises because, unlike convolutional layers, fully connected layers do not share weights. As a result, each component of the output CBE vector is controlled by its own set of learnable parameters that influence only that specific component.

A direct consequence of this is that if the last $K$ elements of a CBE target vector are always zero during training, which is plausible since most target values during training are significantly smaller than the maximum bin value ($b_D$), a BinConv model with a fully connected output layer may trivially learn to predict zeros for those components. Consequently, it fails to extrapolate to higher values during testing beyond the range observed in training.

